## ABSTRACT
In recent years, since water pollution has aroused great public concern, various carbon materials have already been widely applied for water treatment. In this respect, tremendous effort has been made to provide different synthesis methods of carbon materials. Among all carbon materials, metal-organic framework (MOF) derived carbon has always been favored as it possesses several appealing merits such as high specific surface area, large pore volume, and outstanding chemical stability. This review presents the latest development of MOFs as templates and precursors for the fabrication of various carbon materials, including porous carbon, nanocarbon, and graphene, which are pyrolyzed at different temperatures. The article also emphasizes on their future trends and perspectives on the application of water treatment.
## KEYWORDS
metal-organic framework (MOF) derived carbon, synthetic methods, organic pollutants adsorption, adsorption of metal ions, capacitive deionization
## 1 Introduction
With the fast development of urbanization and industrialization, the world has suffered from more serious water resource shortage and pollution. According to the World Water Development Report, this disconcerting dilemma is predicted to deteriorate sharply by 2050, when appropriately a quarter of human will suffer from water shortages more or less. So today much effort has been made in water treatment [1–6]. Nowadays, common methods of treating pollutant water can be summed up including physical (membrane filtration, adsorption, etc.), biological (biological aerated filters, activated sludge, etc.), and chemical (oxidation, precipitation, etc.) methods and so on. For example, membrane filtration technology maintains a significant role. Because carbon nanotubes (CNTs) have large surface area, high aspect ratio, and fast water transport and are extensively used [7, 8]. But, this method faces predicament when it comes to expensive filter membrane materials. Precipitation is also a practical method in water treatment for its striking removal efficiency, especially metal ions [9]. But it also has disadvantages like increasing disposal and long purification time. Besides, the advanced oxidation processes like classical Fenton reaction enjoy good reduction, while suffering from high sludge production [10]. Therefore, the metal-organic framework (MOF) derived carbon materials are expected to be emerging materials for treating water pollution. They can be utilized in the production of filter membrane, adsorbent, capacitive deionization (CDI) electrodes and so on for the accessibility, remarkable efficiency, and easy recyclability [11–16].MOF, constructed from metal ions or metal ion clusters and bridging organic linkers, is a type of functional porous material. It exhibits conventional crystalline lattices, relatively well-defined pore structures, and superior features [17]. Nonetheless, MOFs face critical problems like inherent instability, the existence of only micropores, and the collapsible structure, which limit their applications [18–20]. In order to overcome these disadvantages, the derived carbon materials of MOFs have recently received more and more attention [20]. Pyrolysis can optimize the distribution and uniformity of pore size, so the derived carbon materials generally possess high porosity, uniform and adjustable pore sizes, and well-demarcated active site characteristics [17]. Besides, adjusting temperature for the thermal decomposition of MOF precursors can control morphologies of the derived carbon composites [21, 22]. Due to the above characteristics, MOF derived carbon materials have promising future as raw materials for various water treatment [23, 24]. Therefore, there have been several studies about applying MOF derived carbon materials for the adsorption of pollutants in water [20, 25, 26]. Besides, improving physical and chemical properties of MOF derived carbon usually rests in the introduction of heteroatom such as N, P, and S [27]. These atoms can be easily realized by placing the MOFs in a particular environment with these elements, such as dimethyl sulfoxide (for S) and glyphosine and triarylphosphine (for P) [28]. For instance, the addition of N can adjust the pore size flexibly, affect the dielectric property of the graphitic carbon, catalyze the activity of the reactants [29, 30], and improve the ability to pollution adsorption [31]. The introduction of F can improve the capacity and cycling stability of MOF derived porous carbon, which is the main contributor to the excellent performance in sewage filtering [32]. The P and N duple dopants lead to stratified mesopore–micropores, superior conductivity,
MOF, constructed from metal ions or metal ion clusters and bridging organic linkers, is a type of functional porous material. It
exhibits conventional crystalline lattices, relatively well-defined pore structures, and superior features [17]. Nonetheless, MOFs face critical problems like inherent instability, the existence of only micropores, and the collapsible structure, which limit their applications [18–20]. In order to overcome these disadvantages, the derived carbon materials of MOFs have recently received more and more attention [20]. Pyrolysis can optimize the distribution and uniformity of pore size, so the derived carbon materials generally possess high porosity, uniform and adjustable pore sizes, and well-demarcated active site characteristics [17]. Besides, adjusting temperature for the thermal decomposition of MOF precursors can control morphologies of the derived carbon composites [21, 22]. Due to the above characteristics, MOF derived carbon materials have promising future as raw materials for various water treatment [23, 24]. Therefore, there have been several studies about applying MOF derived carbon materials for the adsorption of pollutants in water [20, 25, 26]. Besides, improving physical and chemical properties of MOF derived carbon usually rests in the introduction of heteroatom such as N, P, and S [27]. These atoms can be easily realized by placing the MOFs in a particular environment with these elements, such as dimethyl sulfoxide (for S) and glyphosine and triarylphosphine (for P) [28]. For instance, the addition of N can adjust the pore size flexibly, affect the dielectric property of the graphitic carbon, catalyze the activity of the reactants [29, 30], and improve the ability to pollution adsorption [31]. The introduction of F can improve the capacity and cycling stability of MOF derived porous carbon, which is the main contributor to the excellent performance in sewage filtering [32]. The P and N duple dopants lead to stratified mesopore–micropores, superior conductivity,
and cycling stability [33]. As a result, we can rationally design strategy of MOF template to prepare heteroatom-doped carbon materials, so as to open a promising way to develop high-efficiency adsorbents for removing waste water pollutant. Metal atoms also exert crucial influence on the properties of MOF deuterogenic carbon materials including the catalytic graphitization of Ni, Co, and Mn atoms and pore-forming effect of Cd and Zn atoms, and so on [34]. Incorporation of trace amounts of metallic Co nanoparticles into the N-doped carbon contributes to its enhanced electrocatalytic activity and stability [35]. Fe and other typical ferromagnetic metals endow the composite [36–38]. It is believed that the anisotropy energy of composite would remarkably increase such as superior oxygen reduction reaction (ORR) activity [24–26]. The formation of crystalline Ni clusters might facilitate the formation of more crystalline carbon when carbonizing at higher temperatures [39]. In a word, these composites are ideal materials for water purification.
By now, many excellent reviews have been reported concerning MOF derived carbon materials and their functional application in water purification including electro-sorption, adsorption of metal ions, and so on [24, 40–44]. For example, an overview described porous carbon (PC) from different precursors and some applications as an adsorbent for wastewater treatment industries [45]. Three modification methods of MOF derived carbon, namely heteroatom doping, morphology control, and Faradaic material doping are summarized and provide new ideas for the industrialization of MOF derived carbon for CDI [46]. On the basis of previous researches, this review aims to provide a more comprehensive summary of the extraction of different MOF derived carbon such as porous carbon, carbon nanotube, graphite, and their composites doped with nonmetal elements and metal elements. Furthermore, this review systematically introduces the high performance behavior in water treatment taking advantage of these MOF derivatives (Scheme 1). As shown in Schemes 2(a) and 2(b), the researches about MOF derived carbon have sprung up in several years and its application is mainly in water treatment about CDI and metal pollution. As seen in Scheme 2(c), the
development of MOF derived carbon and its representative application in water pollution are intuitively expressed. Finally, the review puts forwards several vital future directions for the synthesis of MOF derived carbon materials.
## 2 Synthesis of MOF derived carbon
## 2.1 MOF derived porous carbon
Porous carbon materials enjoy proper carbon content, unique morphologies, governable porous structures, and outstanding chemical, thermal, and mechanical stabilities [47–49]. Therefore, PC can be widespreadly utilized in many fields, such as clean energy storage [50–52], gases separation [53–55], and catalyst supports [56–58]. With MOF-5 as a template, Xu and his coworkers yielded two-dimensional (2D) \(\mathrm{NiTe}_{2}/\mathrm{N}\) -doped graphitic carbon nanosheets through Ni-MOF in 2008. This testified that MOF can be an ideal choice as a precursor to prepare PC [59].
Recently, it has been successfully proved that one-step pyrolysis of a MOF as the only origin of carbon is another approach for preparing PC due to its high carbon contents. Compared with other methods, this technique is more simple and feasible since it contains a single calcination step. Besides, precise regulation of thermolysis conditions can reserve the excellent characters of MOFs and optimize surface area and porosity characters at the same time [60]. Table 1 shows textural features of MOF-derived PC including favorably high surface area and pore volume. MOFs precursors and synthesis process have a great influence on the final porosity and shape properties of deuterogenic carbon [60, 61].
## 2.1.1 Pure porous carbon
MOFs are ideal candidates to synthesize PC via direct pyrolysis of the precursor because of the good characteristics of the derivatives [62]. So far, plentiful researches have demonstrated that simple carbonization of MOFs is a practical way to prepare carbon [63, 64]. The pore and shape adjustable characteristics of derived carbon rely on the original MOFs and the pyrolysis environment to a great extent.
Zn-based MOFs with imidazole as the ligands have been used as typical precursors, among which zeolitic imidazolate framework
8 (ZIF-8) is one of the representatives. It can be transformed into highly PCs with specific surface areas and a relatively wide pore size distribution via simple calcination, partly due to the reduction and volatilization of \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) [13]. For example, Salunkhe's group synthesized a PC via direct carbonization of ZIF-8. The precursor ZIF was heated under flowing \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) gas at \(800~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and decomposed into powders. Then, the powder was calcined for \(5\,\textrm{h}\) . After cooling to normal temperature,thesamplewaswashedwith \(10\%\) HFfor \(24\,\mathrm{~h~}\) to eliminate the \(Z\mathrm{n}\) (Fig. 1(a)). The resulting PC was well uniformed with an average size about \(1\ \upmu\mathrm{m}\) and had large pore size about \(1\ \mathrm{nm}\) . Besides, the surface area of PC reached up to \(1,523~\mathrm{~m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) . Figure 1(b) demonstrated a scanning electron microscopy (SEM) image of nanoporous carbon (NPC). It was clearly shown that these particles were nicely uniformed with an average size about \(1\ \mathrm{~mm}\) . Figure 1(c) showed its high magnification SEM image, where each particulate polyhedral possessed sharp edges and excellent symmetry. The SEM and transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images (Figs. 1(c) and 1(d)) revealed that the derived carbon had the primitive form of ZIF-8 and polyhedral shapes, allowing us to adjust the size of particles by regulating the carbonization conditions [65]. In addition, the size effect of the MOF template on the representation
of PC was carefully investigated. The template Zn-based MOFs and Zn-based MOFs with an adding surface active agent were heated at target temperature of 800, 900, and \( 1{,}000\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) severally for \( 2\,\mathrm{{h}} \) under Ar environment and the resultant PCs were denoted as C-B-800, C-S-800, C-B-900, C-S-900, C-B-1000, and C-S-1000 (Fig. 1(f)). The outcome suggested that C-S-900 showed the highest surface area, whereas C-S-800 showed the lowest (Fig. 1(h)). This phenomenon can be owed to the following factors: the reaction temperature and particle sizes. For one thing, the pyrolysis temperature is too high and leads to the collapse of the nanoporous structure. For another thing, the pyrolysis temperature is too low to make the organic template completely decomposed [93]. The \( 800\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) was not hot enough to carbonize the sacrificial templates into notably PCs. Nevertheless, because of the reduction of \( \mathrm{ZnO} \) and \( \mathrm{Zn} \) at \( 900\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \), the carbon networks can be adequately expanded as shown from the SEM image (Fig. 1(e)). But higher temperature like \( 1{,}000\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) can cause the agglomeration of PC particles, thus decreasing the porosity [78]. Another research reported that Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) surface area of MOF derived PCs followed the order PC-800 \( (885\,\mathrm{\m^{2}{\cdot}g^{-1}}) \) \( < \) PC-1200 \( (1,590\ \mathrm{~m^{2}{\cdot}g^{-1}}) \) \( < \) PC-1000 \( (1,855\ \mathrm{~m^{2}{\cdot}g^{-1}}) \), which confirmed the reported results [13]. Moreover, varying the pyrolytic pressure can also have an effect on the sizes of pores and cavities [94]. For instance, a Zn-based MOF was employed as a precursor and a simply thermal treatment was used to design and prepare PC with multifaceted structures. The result displayed that either pyrolyzed in vacuum or under normal pressure, the pores and cavities of the PC were both spherical and conterminous. However, the former pores and cavities were smaller and denser than those of the latter. In addition, the vacuum atmosphere can improve the gasification quality to obtain cleaner carbon materials [62].
Apart from ZIFs, other MOFs can also play the role of ideal templates and precursors. MOF-5 has the highest aromatic and can accordingly generate target carbon with high surface area [23]. The thermal decomposition characteristics of PC at the point temperature of 500 to \( 1{,}000\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) were further discussed. The obtained PC were recorded as PC-500, PC-700, PC-850, PC-900, and PC-1000, respectively. The surface area of MOF-5 increased to 322.34, 336.37, and 607.67 \( \mathrm{m^{2}{\cdot}g^{-1}} \) after carbonization at 500, 700, and \( 850\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \). The outcome pointed out that the surface area of PC-900 surprisingly mushroomed to \( 1,563{\cdot}90\ \mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1} \), much larger than that of activated carbon (AC) \( (990{\cdot}87\ \mathrm{~m^{2}{\cdot}g^{-1}}) \). This can be attributed to the reduction and removal of \( \mathrm{ZnO} \) and the fabrication of many pores during the pyrolysis course. Nevertheless, further increase in temperature led to the collapse of carbon skeleton, so the surface area of PC-1000 decreased to \( 1,499.36\ \mathrm{m^{2}{\cdot}g^{-1}} \) (Figs. 1(i) and 1(j)) [82]. In addition to controlling the characteristics of pore morphology via adjusting the carbonization temperature, the intervention of a second template presents another convenient method for achieving MOF-derived PC with various porosities. By doing so, the obtained layered carbon contains macropores, mesopores, and/or micropores, which can benefit the mass transport and improve surface area [95]. Xu's team succeeded in obtaining layered PCs with adjustable pore structures firstly from \( \mathrm{Al-MIL-101-NH_{2}} \). The size of \( \mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3} \) was relevant with the amount of added \( \mathrm{Cu}^{2+} \) before carbonization, which can be further applied for generating hierarchical micro-/meso-/macropores PCs without impurity atoms after postprocessing. It was noteworthy that the added \( \mathrm{Cu}^{2+} \) can transform into Cu particles by in situ as pore-forming agents, which caused the phase and modification of \( \mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3} \) from MOFs, thus forming PC with different porosities [95]. NPC possessed lots of nanopores and were also successfully prepared [66, 93, 96]. This structure is very favorable for storing charges and the existence of proper ion diffusion pathways in PC is significant for excellent power property [74].
