## 2.1.2 Incorporation of nonmetal elements into porous carbon
MOFs have gained wide currency as attractive precursors for the synthesis of PCs incorporated of heteroatoms such as N, S, and P. Abundant heteroatom doping is also considered as a powerful strategy for performance optimization [97]. These different types of heteroatom doped PC could be simply obtained by direct pyrolysis of corresponding MOFs or introducing into the samples through the pyrolysis process in different atmospheres [29, 98]. Compared with conventional methods, nonmetal heteroatom-doped PC derived from MOF possesses desirable mesoporous structure, which is beneficial to the rapid transfer and diffusion of reaction substances. In term of heteroatoms, N atom stands out
for its five electron structure and appropriate atomic radius, which are proved to have the advantages of highly-dispersed N, high surface area, controllable structure, and so on. These endow N atoms have high performance and high efficiency, and are widely used in water treatment, battery, and electrocatalysis. In Xu's study, he chose ZIF-67, which had rich N-containing, as the MOF precursor to obtain nitrogen-doped porous carbon (N-doped PC) via a simple low temperature carbonization step [99]. With the continuous improvement of technology, there are two universal synthesis strategies for N-PCs derived from MOFs. The first one is the direct carbonization of N-containing MOFs like ZIF-8, which has high N content (247 wt. \(\%\) ). Their direct carbonization is called "in situ" N doping. The second strategy is introducing N into catalysts by using an additional N resource like \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) (ammonia gas), acetonitrile, etc. Meanwhile, MOF as a carbon source provides large surface area and tunable porosity. Ma reported a new improved method of pyrolyzing ZIF-8 to gain MOF derived carbon with more mesoporous structure and larger size. As shown in route 1 in Fig. 2(a), after treatment of single carbonization, ZIF-8 was directly transformed into carbon with microporous. The first method had been widely practiced [100–103]. The graphitic N content in N-doped carbon was measured around \(15\%\) , \(20\%\) , \(34\%\) , and \(80\%\) with higher pyrolysis temperature [102]. A more complex hollow bamboo-shaped nanofiber NPC has been reported. Cai et al. successfully prepared a bamboo-like nanofiber hollow structure of conductive carbon nitride (c-CN) material by employing the Ni-MOF as a precursor and a N source to obtain adequate N-doped c-CN [104]. However, hollow structures often suffer from defects such as low
tapping density, high cast of templates, and sophisticated procedures for impurities removal. To overcome these disadvantages, Chen explored a practical carbonization way to fabricate hollow N-doped carbon nanofibers. ZIF-8 template was implanted into electrospun polyacrylonitrile and further heated, the mixture was converted into interconnected N-doped carbon hollow nanoparticles. Because of the participation of polyacrylonitrile, the size of the pores limited ranging between 3 and \(4~\mathrm{nm}\) , surpassing samples gained by homogeneously heating ZIF-8. In addition, polyacrylonitrile also improved the surface area of N-decorated carbon nanofibers [105].
To strive for N-decorated mesoporous carbon (NMC) with better characteristics, cheap \(\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{SiO}_{3}\) was utilized as a catalyst with ZIF-8 as a template. Grinded \(\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{SiO}_{3}\) could be inserted into ZIF-8 to form the mixture. In carbonization process, the application of \(\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{SiO}_{3}\) manufactured a mesoporous structure and had a positive role in boosting carbon residual rate. This original method not only made reaction come true under mild conditions through activation operation, but also lowered the cost since \(\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{SiO}_{3}\) can be easily obtained as a common inorganic silicate existing in sandy soil. To further illustrate the microstructure of NC, NMC-0.5, and fine catalysis of \(\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{SiO}_{3}\) , the SEM and TEM images were shown in Fig. 2. It was clearly shown that utilization of \(\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{SiO}_{3}\) had ineffective effect on morphology of ZIF-8, because the SEM images of NC (Fig. 2(b)) and NMC-0.5 (Fig. 2(d)) all possessed a semblable dodecahedral shape. Second, featured with larger diameter, NMC-0.5 (120 nm) (Fig. 2(e)) presented richer porous structure than that of NC (Fig. 2(c)) on account of the support of \(\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{SiO}_{3}\) during pyrolysis. Third, the content of \(\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{SiO}_{3}\) had effect
on the morphology of ZIF-8 as the SEM images of NMC-0.3 (Fig. 2(f)) and NMC-1 (Fig. 2(g)) showing that the derivants remained a dodecahedral shape [106].
Moreover, N content and species have great effect on working efficiency of the derived carbon. So, in Wang's study, three MOFs with varying N content (ZIF-8: \(24.7\ \mathrm{\wt.\%}\), \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-53: \(6.28\ \mathrm{wt.\%}\), and IRMOF-3: \(5.16\ \mathrm{wt.\%}\)) were carbonized to prepare NPCs with different N content. Different carbonization temperatures were used to synthesize the N-PCs with varying nitrogen species. Figure 2(b) shows the structure of different N species in NPC. With ZIF-8 as the same template, the X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) spectra of these NPCs (Figs. 2(i)–2(k)) show that N-PCs under different pyrolysis temperatures (donated as \(\mathrm{NPC}_{\mathrm{ZIF-8-x}}\)) had different graphitic N content and took the lead in N-PC under \(1{,}000\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). When using different MOF templates, NPCs from ZIF-8 had maximum content of graphitic N \((3.74\ \mathrm{at.\%})\) followed by those from IRMOF-3 \((2.76\ \mathrm{at.\%})\) and \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-53 \((1.77\ \mathrm{at.\%})\) (Fig. 2(l)). The N-PC from ZIF-8 maintained the rhombic dodecahedra of ZIF-8, but the average diameter decreased to around \(170\ \mathrm{nm}\), nearly half of its precursor (Figs. 2(m) and 2(n)). The specific surface areas of N-PCs from ZIF-8, \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-53, and IRMOF-3 were calculated to be 998, 787, and \(1{,}068\ \mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), respectively, which provided rich active sites [29].
Apart from the direct carbonization of N-bearing MOFs, N can also be introduced by adopting an extra N source with MOFs only furnishing carbon structure to improve surface area and porosity. MIL-101 and pyrrole monomer were utilized to prepare derived carbon electrocatalyst, which had high N doping \((2.53\ \mathrm{at.\%})\) and effective graphite and pyridinium N doping \((86.6\%)\) [107]. Wang's team succeeded in heating MOF and urea at 800, 900, and \(1{,}000\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) in Ar condition [108]. As precursors, MOFs were carbonized at 800, 900, and \(1{,}000\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) with urea as an extra N resource, thus forming the N-PC-800, N-PC-900, and N-PC-1000. The close observation of N-PC-900 furthermore demonstrated that the PC had a hollow uniform ball structure with radius around \(4.13\ \mathrm{nm}\) [108]. \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) is also a rich N source. Compared with direct pyrolysis of MOFs, the in situ ammonia treatment enables MOF derived carbon with layered mesoporous structure [68]. Dual-ligand MOF can be an ideal precursor for its superior properties of N-PC. Zhang's group recently demonstrated a feasible way by using dual-ligand MOF. Dual-ligand MOF powder was heated firstly to \(200\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and further \(900\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(2\ \mathrm{h}\) under \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) atmosphere provided by triethylenediamine, and finally cooled down to indoor temperature. After complete removal of \(\mathrm{Zn}\), a N-doped rod-shaped porous carbon from dual-ligand MOFs (MNPC) was gained. These gained MNPC had a rod-like structure with many holes made up of rough particles, increasing the surface area. Another advantage was that meso/macropores existed in rods, accelerating the transport and diffusion of salt ions. Compared with other MNPC synthesized from other methods, this way had two strengths. For one thing, MNPC increased the specific surface area of the material thanks to coarse particles with many holes. For another thing, this method provided the convenience because by controlling the proportion of two ingredients, carbon composites with desirable surface area and N content can be readily harvested, which were of great use in various fields such as achieving the highest removal capacity in water treatment [109].
In addition to N atom, insertion of other nonmetal elements like P and S also shows unique characteristics. In terms of P, Li inserted red P into ZIF-8-derived nanoporous carbon to gain MOF derived porous carbon nanorods with P. They showed a striking service life after 1,000 cycles in sodium-ion batteries. However, because of the limitation of derived carbon, the restricted P content \((22.6\ \mathrm{wt.\%})\) is still a challenge [110]. A related
survey reported a very facile, one-step of pyrolysis of ZIF-8 after using a mixed-solvent strategy to acquire ZIF-8 nanorods. Then the obtained materials were converted into carbon nanorods via pyrolysis and followed with red phosphorous introduction. By controlling the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) content in \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}/\mathrm{N},\mathrm{N}\)-Dimethylformamide (DMF) mixed solvent in this process (Figs. 3(b)–3(d)), the diameter of ZIF-8 was adjusted from tens of nanometers to hundreds of nanometers even to micron size, increasing the content of P in the subsequent impregnation treatment [111]. Powder, holding abundant storage, is generally embedded into PC to gain S-decorated carbon with mesoporous structure. MOF-5, simultaneously providing high surface area and adjustable structure, had been utilized as an ideal precursor to fabricate hierarchical PC. The synthesis procedure was exhibited in Fig. 3(h). A mixture of MOF-5 and sulfur powder with weight ratio of 1:1 was calcined at \(155\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) under Ar condition for \(6\ \mathrm{h}\) to insert S into the channels of MOF-5 totally. After further pyrolysis for \(4\ \mathrm{h}\) at \(950\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), the removal of \(\mathrm{Zn}\) and S mingling process was stimulated. Finally, the residue of sulfur-doped mesoporous carbon was respectively washed with HCl and deionized water. For comparison with the pure PC prepared by the same precursor under the same condition, both of them remained the original morphology and uniformly porous structure of MOF-5. However, more severe structural deformation took place in the cubic morphology of pure PC than S-doping ones, proving that S addition can improve structural stability (Figs. 3(i)–3(n)) [112]. Microporous carbon polyhedrons with a total sulfur content ranging from \(43\ \mathrm{wt.\%}\) to \(63\ \mathrm{wt.\%}\) were also prepared [113].
It is beyond doubt that the structure of co-doping with multiple nonmetallic heteroatoms can boost properties in some respects. By simple pyrolysis of ZIF-8, hydrothermal treatment, and being immersed with \(\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{F}\) solution and pyrolysis, F/N co-doped PC materials were obtained [32]. Another \(\mathrm{N/O}\) co-adulteration PC had been successfully synthesized through microporous or nonporous MOFs with high N containing heated at 700, 800, and \(900\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). The products largely retained the original octahedron shape from the parent [114]. Interestingly, Bio-MOFs can be used as precursors to fabricate PCs. A research reported that the PC achieved by carbonization of Bio-MOF-1 firstly (Fig. 3(e)). The characterization of Bio-MOF-1-derived carbons (BMDCs) turned out to be highly PC. The morphologies slightly changed such as the decreased widths after thermolysis and lavation with HCl (Fig. 3(g)). To better figure out the features of O and N in the BMDCs, XPS images of BMDC-6 h and BMDC-12 h are shown in Fig. 3(f). The images also confirmed the existence of O and N atoms in the BMDCs [11]. B, N co-doped PC originated from Bio-MOF-1 showed that N and B elements were evenly distributed in PC. Since B has lower electronegativity than C, it enhances positive charge density and the activation of C atoms [115]. Researches show that N and S together can effectively offer better active sites than N or S alone [116, 117]. In detail, ZIF-8 was turned into N-doped carbon after carbonization at \(1{,}000\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). Under thermal treatment with \(\mathrm{NH}_{4}\), the product \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\)-C-x particles formed a much rougher surface. Subsequently, \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\)-C-7 nanocarbon got the nod because it fully inherited the basic appearance of the ZIF-8 precursor and got enough defective sites during the carbonization and \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) treatment. Finally, the N,S-co-doped carbon \((\mathrm{N},\mathrm{S}-\mathrm{NH}_{3}-\mathrm{C}-7)\) was fabricated by introducing S into the \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\)-C-7 carbon skeleton. The scanning TEM (STEM) image confirmed that N and S atoms were finely inserted into carbon skeleton and uniformly distributed. Moreover, along with the introduction of S atom, the obtained carbon had a much rougher surface. This method provided guidelines for preparing of N,S-doped MOF derived carbon decorated with efficient active sites [117].
## 2.1.3 Incorporation of metal elements into porous carbon
Apart from non-metallic elements, metal elements can also be inserted into MOF derived carbon via templates with objective elements. This step can enhance the integrated performances of carbon-based materials such as hydrophilicity, electronic conductivity, and stability [46,118]. Through self-assembly of transition metal elements or clusters and organic ligands, MOFs own unique periodic network structure. So they are ideal templates to build magnetic carbon [119–121]. For example, favose \(\mathrm{Co/C}\) composites with rich hierarchical pores were obtained through in situ pyrolysis treatment under Ar atmosphere [122]. Besides, high surface area and uniform doping are deemed as significant indicators to optimize the properties of carbon materials. The derived carbon possesses uniform heteroatom decoration and preserves the ordered structure and high porosity of MOFs. As a result, these products have double advantages of heteroatomic atoms and MOFs [123]. Moreover, the incorporation of metal heteroatoms such as \(\mathrm{Ag}\) and \(\mathrm{Cu}\) into carbon can effectively regulate their intrinsic characteristics, such as partial chemical features, electronic properties, and mechanical characteristics. Recently, a facile method for achieving electrode bar with sufficiently good properties due to the introduction of \(\mathrm{Ag}\) atom was proposed. The obtained Ag-doped ZIF-derived porous carbon \((\mathrm{Ag/ZCs})\) had specific hollow structure and strong redox activity on account that Ag nanoparticles took up the position of the primitive cavities and lowered the relevant pore volume and specific surface area. The detailed synthesis method was as follows. \(40~\mathrm{mL}\) of \(0.01\mathrm{~M~Ag}(\mathrm{NH}_{3})_{2}\mathrm{OH}\) was added and mixed with the
hollow ZIF-8 (HZIF-8) solution, the product was heated at \(80\,^{\circ}\!C\) to gain Ag-decorated hollow ZIF-8 (Ag/HZIF-8). The as-obtained brown precipitates decomposed under \(900~^{\circ}\!C\) for \(3\mathrm{~h~}\) under \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) flows. The ultimate \(\mathrm{Ag/ZCs}\) were gained after removing \(Z\mathfrak{n}\) with \(10\;\mathrm{wt.}\%\;\mathrm{HCl}\) solution (Fig. 4(a)). Figure 4(b) exhibited that Ag was adequately stuck on the surface and the cavities of HZIF-8. After calcination, \(\mathrm{Ag/ZCs}\) stayed the same with their precursors but their particle sizes of the hollow cubes decreased to \(100\ \mathrm{nm}\) with \(\mathrm{Ag}\) about \(20{-}30\ \mathrm{nm}\) (Figs. 4(d) and 4(e)). Because the as-gained \(\mathrm{Ag/ZCs}\) enjoyed special hollow cavities and strong redox activity, they showed excellent deionization capacity in CDI process, which will be detailed explained below [124]. Similarly, different pyrolysis temperatures during the process can exert an effect on the properties of the products [125–127]. Excessive heating may lead to the structural destruction of original MOFs and the partial or completely collapse of porous structure. Besides, temperature also observably affects the condition of decorated atoms, which are representative active sites for reaction [194]. In a research, multiple porous carbon-based adsorbents were fabricated by using Cu-BTC as the carbon source and precursor via calcination with diverse reaction temperatures (400, 600, and \(800\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) . After calcination, the typical octahedral morphology of the Cu-BTC particles could not be seen. C-Cu-BTC-400 particles possessed a pyramid configuration. As the carbonization temperature increased to \(600~^{\circ}\mathrm{C},\) particles with pyramidal shape disappeared. Instead, the irregular particles took shape. The surfaces of C-Cu-BTC-600 kept coarse due to the evident pores. Once the pyrolysis temperature increased to \(800\ ^{\circ}{C},\) the sample with stratified structure appeared uneven distribution. Temperature also leads to the difference in
BET surface area and pore volume. C-Cu-BTC-400 presented the minimum BET surface area of \(118.49\;\mathrm{~m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) and the lowest micropore volume. C-Cu-BTC-600 showed a higher BET surface area about \(630.97\;\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) and a larger total pore volume up to 1.06 \(\mathrm{cm}^{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\). This occurred because the increasing temperature provided enough condition for successful carbon bed formation. However, the carbon skeleton started to decompose when the calcination temperature was above \(600\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). So, C-Cu-BTC-800 showed a lower surface area around \(383.14\;\mathrm{~m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) and pore volume around \(0.45\;\mathrm{cm}^{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) [128].
Apart from the direct incorporation of metal heteroatoms using a second chemical reagent during the carbonization of MOFs, bimetallic metal-organic frameworks (BMOFs) have been testified as exemplary precursors for PCs [129–133]. Single MOFs show limited abilities owing to unitary metal ions and simple organic linkers. Single-metal ZIFs consist of cobalt ions, such as ZIF-67 commonly displays a mesoporous structure with highly graphited, but a relatively small surface area and N doping [134]. By precisely tuning the components in the BMOF, the optimal carbon can be systematically yielded. Tang and his co-workers prepared core-shell ZIFs by utilizing ZIF-8 with ZIF-67 coated through epitaxial growth. In the carbonization process, the Melm coordinated with \(Z\mathfrak{n}^{2+}\) was turned into \(_\mathrm{N}\) doped carbon and remained the intrinsic micropores. On contrary, the Melm with \({\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}\) commonly became graphitic carbon [135]. Besides, characteristics of BMOF decided upon the proportion of the dual metal [136]. As a result, it can be utilized to obtain PCs with different particle sizes by means of adjustments of the ratio of two metal atoms [137, 138]. The feasibility of this method was proved by Ding et al. They used BMOFs from the ZIF-8 and ZIF-67, which possessed \(Z\mathfrak{n}\) and Co elements. Through calcination under an Ar flow at \(1{,}100\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C},\) Zn and Co metal were reduced from \(Z\mathfrak{n}\) and Co ions and \(Z\mathfrak{n}\) was further evaporated at higher temperature close to its boiling point (Fig. 4(f)). As a result, they successfully gained PC with \(Z\mathfrak{n}\) , Co, and both doped at one time. Comparing
the SEM images of BMOF-ZnCo-3 (Fig. 4(g)), PC-Zn (Fig. 4(h)), PC-Co (Fig. 4(i)), and PC-ZnCo-3 (Fig. 4(j)), the surface of PC-Zn was very smooth. Nevertheless, the PC-Co had a distinctly coarse and curved surface. The surface of PC-ZnCo-3 resembled to that of PC-Co, showing a rugged texture. Moreover, the BET surface areas of PC-Co, PC-Zn, and PC-ZnCo-3 were 124, 609, and \(350\;\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) separately. Increasing Zn content in precursors was a practical adjustment for optimizing the surface areas of deuterogenic carbon materials [139]. Li obtained CoNi-doped carbon materials with optimized structure through regulating the insert ratio of Co and Ni to 0.25:0.75 [137]. Similarly, adding a pointed molar ratio \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) or \(\mathrm{Mg}^{2+}\) (\(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) or \(\mathrm{Mg}^{2+}=2:1\)) when making ZIF-67, \(\mathrm{NiCo}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{C}\) and \(\mathrm{MgCo}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{C}\) were prepared after heating \(\mathrm{NiCo}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{C}\) and \(\mathrm{MgCo}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{C}\) were prepared and displayed large surface area and bimetallic active sites [140].
## 2.2 MOF derived carbon nanotube
The thermal transition of MOFs generates a wide range of carbon nanotubes with desirable properties like adjustable pores that enable them promising potential in various fields.
## 2.2.1 Incorporation of metal elements into carbon nanotubes
With large surface areas, carbon nanotubes have been utilized as stroma to load metal particles so as to avoid aggregation, optimize surface area, and improve competence of catalysis. The common fabrication of matrix and loading treatment requires complex conditions and operations. However, MOFs are made of metal and organic linkers, which can be easily transformed to get metal-doped carbon nanomaterials via carbonization under inert atmosphere at specific temperatures. As a result, these MOF derived carbonaceous materials have bright future thanks to their simple production and duple peculiarities both from carbon and metals.
Xu's group selected MOF-74 as a precursor to generate carbon nanorods with spherical superstructure after carbonization in Ar,
which maintained the primordial chestnut-shell-shaped superstructure with one-dimensional (1D) PC nanorods on the shell. Benefiting from this structure, globose superstructure of carbon nanorods was further decorated with ultrafine Pd nanoparticles [141]. Recently, Xu's team synthesized a more complex 2D nanoribbon superstructure of graphene nanocages whose surface area was up to \(72\;\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) and approximately \(4\;\mathrm{nm}\) pore size by a direct thermal exfoliation of 1D MOF nanoroads for the first time. During this process, interlinked graphene nanocages were self-assembled to form a belt-shaped superstructure with some metallic Ni nanoparticles decorated on the surface. The interconnected graphene nanocages provided more effective active sites for reactions and facilitated the mass transfer. Because of this unique property, carbon material with such nanostructures showed obvious superiority in electrocatalysis application [142]. Combining the magnetic metal with carbonaceous materials can be a practical way to solve disadvantages such as confined absorbing bandwidth and high density due to the synergistic effect
of two constituents. For example, \(3\;\mathrm{g}\) polyvinyl pyrrolidone (PVP), \(0.3\;\mathrm{g}\;\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}\), and \(0.864\;\mathrm{g}\;\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}\cdot6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) were placed in deionized water and DMF mixed solution at a specified volume proportion of \((60-x:x)\) (\(x\) is the volume of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\)). After subsequent agitation for \(0.5\;\mathrm{h}\), the liquid was heat at \(150\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(10\;\mathrm{h}\) to prepare MOFs. Then, the as-gained Ni-MOF was heated at \(600\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) in Ar environment for \(2\;\mathrm{h}\). The obtained products were denoted as \(\mathrm{Ni}/\mathrm{C}-x\) where \(x=\) volume of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) (0, 10, 20, and \(30\;\mathrm{mL}\)) were contained in precursors. Notably, the morphology of Ni-MOF after calcination was well maintained (Figs. 5(b)–5(e)). But the increase of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) made difference in the sizes of \(\mathrm{Ni}/\mathrm{C}\) spheres. The average size of \(\mathrm{Ni}/\mathrm{C}-x\) (\(x=0\), 10, 20, 30) was about 0.35, 0.55, 1.1, and 1.35 \(\upmu\mathrm{m}\), respectively (Figs. 5(f)–5(i)). TEM measurement was used to confirm that these Ni nanoparticles were fully covered by carbon. In X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns, the \(\mathrm{Ni}/\mathrm{C}-20\) composite showed the highest peak intensity, possessing the best crystallinity. The \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) absorption–deposition isotherms also showed the \(\mathrm{Ni}/\mathrm{C}-20\) composite exhibited the largest BET surface area up to
\(194.85~\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) and the lowest total pore volume of \(0.29~\mathrm{cm}^{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\). By comparison, Ni/C-20 had the relatively best properties. This study demonstrated that appropriate regulation was a practical approach for fabricating good-performance materials [143]. Another magnetic metal Co is also a common heteroatom since conventional ZIF-67 contains many Co nanoparticles. The as-prepared ZIF-67 was carbonized in Ar flows at an appointed temperature. The gained \(\mathrm{Co/C}\) composites were marked as \(\mathrm{Co/C}\) - HS- \(x,\) where \(x\) expressed carbonization temperature. It was found that their average sizes became smaller and appeared a strict decrease when the heat temperature increased. Furthermore, when using acid etching to remove Co particles, the hollow microstructure of these \(\mathrm{Co/C}\) microspheres remained fine mechanical stability, which implied their strong capacity to survive during pressing process in electromagnetic measurement [144]. Besides, Co particles can be leached out from the matrix to a more porous structures with the BET surface area increased 2.5-fold as well as the micro and mesopore sizes [145]. Although the BET surface area decreased dramatically after heat treatment, the resultant Co-doped carbon nanotube still remained high surface area which were up to 386, 391, and \(685.2~\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) [146–148]. By adjusting the concentration of MOF precursors, FeNi nanotubes (NTs) were compared with other MOF derived nanocarbons including FeNi nanosheets (NSs), FeNi nanoflowers (NFs), and FeNi aggregations (AGs) (Fig. 5(j)). After high temperature of heat and local oxidation, the surfaces of both nanosheets and nanoflowers were coarse and crimped. However, the nanoparticles transformed into bamboo-like nanotubes due to the nanoparticles' aggregation and catalytic effects. In contrast, the high concentration of PVP caused the formation of decussate fine nanosheet MOFs, which can further turn into FeNi aggregations after the pyrolysis (Figs. 5(k)–5(n)) [149].
Inert metals such as \(\mathrm{Cu}\) particles have influence on catalytic performance, which suggests that they could promote the ORR activity [150]. The introduction of \(Z\mathfrak{n}\) and \(\mathrm{Ag}\) atoms into nanocarbon can achieve fine chemical and superior photothermal antibacterial efficiency even at low concentrations. For example, the mixture of \(250~\mathrm{mg}\) ZIF and \(39.37~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{AgNO}_{3}\) was used to prepare \(\mathrm{C-Zn/Ag-1}\). The product had \(\mathrm{Ag}\) contents of \(12.59\;\mathrm{wt.}\%\) and radius about \(46\;\mathrm{nm}\). It was obviously found that plenty of \(Z\mathfrak{n}\) and \(\mathrm{Ag}\) particles presented uniform distribution on the surfaces of \(\mathrm{C-Zn/Ag}\) composites, which may release \(Z\mathfrak{n}^{2+}\) and \(\mathrm{Ag^{+}}\) ions during chemical reaction [151].
2.2.2 Incorporation of metal and nonmetal elements into carbon nanotubes
In recent years, much researches have aimed at incorporating dopants into MOF derived CNTs to improve their performance in the all fields [152]. For example, ZIF-67 was heated at the required temperature (700, 800, and \(900\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) to yield N-doped carbon nanotubes (NCNTs). After high-temperature pyrolysis, the scribed polyhedrons in ZIF-67 can be transformed into loose frameworks equipped with twining carbon nanotubes. This further validated that NCNTs inherited the basic profile structure. But its surface became much rougher owing to numerous nanotubes on its surface [30]. The ZIF-67 was carbonized at \(350\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(1.5\,\mathrm{h}\) under the gas mixture atmosphere of \(95\%\) Ar gas and \(5\%\,\mathrm{H}_{2}\) gas and was heated under \(700\ \ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(3.5\ \mathrm{~h~}\) . The obtained products of Co/NCNTs were uniformly mixed with sulfur powder (weight ratio \(=1{:}2\) ) and heated to \(300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(2\,\mathrm{{h}}\) under \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) atmosphere to get \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NCNTs}\) . This carbon nanotube networks derived from MOF interlaced on the surface of framework and \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}\) nanoparticles also attached on the surface. The product possessed merits of higher surface area, larger pore volume, and more concentrated pore size distribution, offering abundant active sites.
Its hollow structure can also accelerate charge transport [153]. Another study revealed the relationship between the amount of iron salt in the template and the yield of carbon nanotubes. The precursor ZIF-8 was stirred for \(24~\mathrm{h}\) with the solution of ferrous sulfate \(\mathrm{(FeSO_{4})}\) (10, 30, and \(60~\mathrm{mg}\) , respectively) and hydrated hydrazine \(\mathrm{(N_{2}H_{4}{\cdot}H_{2}O)}\) \(\mathrm{[0\mL]}\) ). After washing and drying, the precursor powders (named as \(x\%\) Fe-ZIF-8, \(x\) represents different iron amounts) were heated under different temperatures (800, 900, and \(1,000\ \ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) for different time (2, 3, and \(4\,\mathrm{~h~}\) ) in Ar atmosphere (Fig. 6(a)). The products were named as \(x\%\) Fe-N-S CNN. The carbide of ZIF-8 after pyrolysis was named as CN. It was found that the output of carbon nanotubes improved with an increasing amount of iron salt. In the TEM images (Figs. 6(d) and 6(e)), CNTs derived from ZIF-8 perfectly remained the polyhedral morphology, while carbon matrix and nanotubes can be clearly seen in \(6\%\) Fe-N-S CNN (derived from \(500~\mathrm{~mg~}\) ZIF-8 impregnated with \(30~\mathrm{mg}\) iron salts). Carbon nanotubes with \(8\;\mathrm{nm}\) diameter were embedded in the carbon matrix (Fig. 6(f)), possibly resulting in a synergistic effect on the catalytic processes. Benefiting from the porous carbon matrix derived from ZIF-8, \(6\%\) Fe-N-S CNN showed a high surface area of \(974~\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) [154]. A novel bi-functional electrocatalyst of Co-embedded CNT/porous carbon (denoted as Co-CNT/PC) was prepared via a simple pyrolysis of ZIF-67 encapsulated \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) nanoparticles. The detailed synthesis route of Co-CNT/PC was illustrated in Fig. 6(b). Figures \(6(\mathrm{g})\) and 6(h) showed SEM images of Co-CNT/PC. The size of Co-CNT/PC decreased to \(\sim\,400\ \mathrm{\nm}\) due to the decomposition and shrinkage. As expected, CNTs with an average diameter of \(\sim20\ \mathrm{nm}\) could be observed clearly in Fig. 6(h). TEM image of Co-CNT/PC (Fig. 6(i)) clearly showed the presence of CNTs surrounding the carbon sphere. It was worth noting that, during the pyrolysis process, the encapsulated \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) nanoparticles by ZIF-67 were reduced to metallic Co to catalyze the growth of N-doped CNT with the active C and \(_\mathrm{N}\) species as precursors decomposed from MeIM [155].
\(\mathrm{Co/N}\) co-doped CNTs are common ones in terms of metal and nonmetal elements doped together. In a typical process, Co-MOF-74 was transferred into a autoclave and placed in an oven at \(175\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for various time (0.5, 1, 2, 3, 6, and \(12\,\mathrm{{h}}\) ) to gain Co-MOF-74-NT. It was shown that Co-MOF-74-NT possessed large surface area \((848.8\,\mathrm{~m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})\) and pore volume \((0.68\,\mathrm{\cm}^{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})\) . Figure 6(j) reveled Co-MOF-74-NT was overlength with the average diameter around \(66.8\;\mathrm{nm}\) . Besides, the nanochannels in Co-MOF-74-NT shared the wall with six neighboring channels and a nanotube of Co-MOF-74-NT formed one single coordinating framework as a single crystal. Further, \(20~\mathrm{mg}\) Co-MOF-74-NTs and \(0.4~\mathrm{g}\) of dicyandiamide (DCDA) were mixed first heated at \(400\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(3\,\mathrm{h},\) then raised to 600, 700, 800, 900, and \(1,000\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C},\) and maintained at these temperatures for another \(4\,\mathrm{~h~}\) to prepare \(\mathrm{NCo}@\mathrm{CNT-NFT}\) ( \(T\) is the pyrolysis temperature) (Fig. 6(c)). Increasing the pyrolysis temperature resulted in the formation of a three-dimensional (3D) hierarchical dendrite-like nanostructure. Meanwhile, Fig. 6(k) showed that \(\mathrm{Co}@\mathrm{CNFs}\) were wrapped by a lot of CNTs \(\sim\,20\ \mathrm{nm}\) in diameter, hundreds of nanometers to micrometers in length). It is worth mentioning that the pore volumes of \(\mathrm{NCo}@\mathrm{CNT-NFT}\) \((T\ \geq\ 700)\) were largest among carbons obtained from Co-MOF-74 in different morphologies. This was benefited from their small diameter which provided rich sites for the growth of CNTs on the surface. The above results suggested that the Co-MOF-74-NT is a good precursor to fabricate 3D hierarchical carbon nanostructures with a large porosity [156]. Yan reported a facile and controllable synthesis strategy for a series of nickel-cobalt mixed metal phosphide nanotubes for the first time (termed as \(\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{y}P}\) or \(\mathrm{Co_{4}N i_{1}P}\) nanotubes, where \(x\) and \(y\) were the molecularities of Co and Ni in
the MOF precursor) via low-temperature phosphorization from bimetallic MOF precursors [157].
## 2.3 MOF derived graphite
Graphite is made from countless sheets of graphene that are just a carbon atom thick. Graphene, 2D \(\mathrm{sp}^{2}\) hybridized carbon network with special unilaminar structure, shows great potential in many applications. Besides, theoretical studies have demonstrated that the participation of other elements can well modify the features of graphene. In consequence, graphite carbon materials decorated with heteroatoms, especially N, S, Co, and Fe, are probable candidates for the application in various fields like water treatment [158, 159]. Xu's team successfully got MOF-derived hierarchical graphitic carbon structures decorated with Fe and N in 2017. The resultant products possessed high specific surface areas, hierarchical pore structure, and rich surface functionality, optimizing the porous structures and surface features of the graphite carbon materials [160]. The building of MOF derived 2D materials was also explored. Ni-MOF played the role of a template to synthesize \(\mathrm{NiTe}_{2}/\mathrm{N}\)-doped graphitic carbon nanosheets by calefaction and subsequent tellurization [161]. Due to the containing of Co and N elements, ZIFs have been adopted to prepare particular structure and N doping carbon. For instance, a novel graphitic carbon nitride coated with oxygen vacancies-rich ZnO nanocomposites was prepared by direct thermal decomposition of ZIF-8 in air condition [162]. The ZIF-67 precursor was etched with a weak organic acid at room temperature and then heated at \(800\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) in \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) environment to build MOF derived graphitic carbon. In pyrolysis treatment, parts of the organic linkers and the \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) species existed in ZIF-67 were turned into graphitic carbon and Co nanoparticles, respectively [163].
Researches revealed that the Co nanoparticles were regarded as catalysts to promote the fabrication of graphitic carbon while N species served as doping heteroatoms when forming graphite carbon. For instance, the precursor ZIF-67 was heated at \(1{,}100\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) in \(\mathrm{Ar}/\mathrm{H}_{2}\) (95%/5%) and rinsed with \(0.5\,\mathrm{M}\) \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}\). Through the XRD measurement, they confirmed that ZIF-67 was totally transformed into graphitic carbon after removal of Co metal. Besides, the product exhibited the large specific surface area and pore volume [164].
The MOF-precursor strategy is considered as a hopeful strategy to generate a sequence of derived graphite [165]. Nevertheless, the usual carbon from MOF is confronted with problems such as severe resistance and damage in porosity when calcination. In this regard, Gao's group employed a 2D MOF which can facilitate charge transfer and offer abundant available active sites. \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{Co}@\mathrm{two}\)-dimensional ZIF-8- \(x\) \((x=1{-}4\), representing the corresponding amounts of \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) to gain the precursor) was heated at \(800\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and kept in \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) environment. The sample was dipped in \(1\,\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}^{-1}\) HCl for \(12\,\mathrm{h}\), and followed by completely washing using deionized water. Via vacuum drying at \(60\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), 2D MOF-derived N-doped carbon network \((\mathrm{PtCo}@\mathrm{N-GNS}{-}x)\) was obtained. For the purpose of optimization, \(\mathrm{PtCo}@\mathrm{N-GNS}{-}3\) was carbonized at 700, 800, 900, and \(1{,}000\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) to yield the target MOF-derived 2D/3D hierarchical N-doped graphene. TEM image of \(\mathrm{PtCo}@\mathrm{N-GNS}{-}3\) showed that 2D carbonaceous nanosheets were well decorated with quantity of \(\sim10\,\mathrm{nm}\) nanoparticles (Fig. 7(b)). Figure 7(c) showed the featured lattice spacing of 2.03 and \(3.34\,\mathrm{\AA}\) for the (111) plane of Co and the (002) plane of graphene, respectively, proving Co limited in graphitic layers. Slight morphological distortions can be found on the discontinuous graphitic layers (signed in red circle in Fig. 7(c)), suggesting the existence of open channels on the wrappers. Figure 7(d) proved
that metallic \(\mathrm{Pt}_{3}\mathrm{Co}\) was also shown in \(\mathrm{PtCo@N-GNS-3,}\) proving the alloying between Co and Pt during the pyrolysis. Figures 7(e)–7(i) also exhibited the distribution of elements of N-doped graphene nanosheets, from which we can see that the Co and Pt particles were finely distributed, just in fine accordance with the aforesaid XRD results [166]. Apart from Co nanoparticle as the doped atom, \(\mathrm{NiFe@N}\) -doped graphene microtube composites were also prepared through a pyrolysis of the MOF precursor of nickel nitroprussides in \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) condition. By regulating the heating temperature, the radius of the obtained graphene can be adjusted to \(25{-}100\ \mathrm{nm}\) . The sample under \(800~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) had the largest specific surface area of \(160.8\,\mathrm{\m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) [167]. 2D \(\mathrm{NiTe}_{2}/\mathrm{N}\) -doped graphitic carbon nanosheets were also yielded through Ni-MOF. The derived graphite possessed the merits of 2D nanostructures including large specific surface area and abundant active sites [161].
In order to explore the impacts of carbonization temperatures and the surface functionalities of the derived graphite, \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}[\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]_{2}\) spheres were employed as templates for the subsequent pyrolysis process under different temperatures (Fig. 7(j)). The samples gained at pointed temperatures were donated as S-600, S-700, and S-800. The high temperature led to the Fe and Co atoms forming FeCo alloy nanoparticles. At the same time, CN-group linkers acted as C and N sources for the fabrication of N containing graphene. According to the fieldemission SEM (FESEM) results and TEM image (Figs. 7(k) and 7(l)), all the products exhibited a ball-shaped morphology whose radius was around \(300{-}500\ \mathrm{nm}\) . S-600 (Fig. 7(m)) demonstrated that the globular product consisted of small parceled alloy particles. After amplification, it clearly exhibited that the small alloy powder was wrapped in about 5 layers of graphene (Figs. 7(n)–7(p)) [168].
However, high temperature heating is usually completed through instruments such as tubular furnaces, reaction stills, and so on. Recently, Yang et al. proposed a time-saving and accessible
laser-induced strategy for building MOF derived Co-doped graphene at room temperature and air atmosphere with the assistance of laser. Due to the high energy of the laser, the temperature can rise to \(1,700\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C},\) resulting in the transformation of organic ligands into porous graphene. Compared with the common method, this rapid and convenient synthetic route is more beneficial to the massive preparation of MOF-derived graphite without noble-metal. With the assistance of the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) laser, a polyimide film, a conductive substrate for the accommodation of MOFs, was irradiated to form the porous graphene substrate. Next, ZIF-67 in ethanol was evenly immobilized on the substrate and placed under normal temperature and pressure. Once the alcohol was fully evaporated, the template was converted into Co-doped porous graphene. Moreover, increase of laser power favored the increase in Co nanoparticle size and even distribution [169].
## 2.4 Summary
Several strategies have been proposed for preparing MOF derived carbon materials, and calcination is considered as a principle, common, tunable, and effective strategy for the preparation of MOF derived carbon materials [170]. After calefaction in inactive gaseous and then by an acid-etching treatment, MOF derived carbon can be obtained [171, 172]. For example, Cu-MOF designed as core materials can increase openly the number of active sites when calcined at high temperature [173]. Besides, the analysis showed that temperature has great effect on morphology of derived carbon. Calcination of the Co-MOF at \(600~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) favors the formation of \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4},\) while calcination at \(700~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) produced a catalyst composed of metallic Co embedded in a carbon matrix [174]. From the thermogravimetric curve, UiO-66 sample collapsed at \(250~^{\circ}\mathrm{C},\) while thermal decomposition starts at \(450\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) in the oxidative environment and at \(500~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) in the reductive and inert environments [175]. Hydrothermal transformation process is another common one [176, 177]. MOF derived carbon materials
can preserve the original morphology by this way. For example, Zn-MOF-74 derived carbon nanorods possessed a hollow interior assembled from the template [141]. In all, MOF derived carbon has become an indispensable material.
## 3 Applications of MOF derived carbon in water treatment
## 3.1 Absorption of metal ions
As a major contributor to water pollution, toxic metals such as cadmium, lead, and mercury have been a severe environmental problem all over the world due to their adverse threat to the stability of human beings and water ecosystem [178–180]. MOF derived carbon is a kind of well-known absorbing material due to the excellent properties including high surface area and porosity rich surface-active sites [181].
An environmentally-friendly \(\upgamma\)-cyclodextrin MOF-based nanocarbon material was found its effective and efficient capacity to remove \(C\mathrm{d}^{2+}\). In order to maximize the adsorption performance, effects of several factors were examined carefully. First, \(\mathrm{pH}\) is important in adsorption performance because it has something with the surface-charge ionization of adsorbent and the morphology of adsorbing materials. The removal ability of \(\upgamma\)-cyclodextrin MOF-based nanocarbon for \(C\mathrm{d}^{2+}\) was investigated with the \(\mathrm{pH}\) ranging from 1.0 to 8.0 (Fig. 8(e)). Taken \(\mathrm{pH}=4\) as the turning point, the removal of \(C\mathrm{d}^{2+}\) increased dramatically at first but changed markedly when \(\mathrm{pH}\) was from 4.0 to 8.0. So, although the material did not have strict requirement in \(\mathrm{pH}\), but the optimal range was from about 4.0 to 8.0. What is more, the solution concentration is another critical factor for the adsorption efficiency. They studied removal efficiency of the adsorbent
amount in the range of \(0.1{-}2\;\;\mathrm{g}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}^{-1}\) at an original density of \(50\ \mathrm{~\mg{\cdot}L^{-1}}\). Obviously, the removal of \(C\mathrm{d}^{2+}\) strengthened dramatically when the concentration raised from 0.1 to \(0.5\;\mathrm{g}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}^{-1}\), but the continuous increasing in the adsorbent dosage only induced a small variation in the working efficiency. So, \(1\;\mathrm{g}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}^{-1}\) was the optimal density. Third, the influence of concomitant ions is essential as they generally exist in waters in nature. Three kinds of cations \((\mathrm{Na}^{+},\,\mathrm{Ca}^{2+},\,\mathrm{Mg}^{2+})\) and three kinds of anions \((\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{-},\,\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{2-},\,\mathrm{Cl}^{-})\) were selected and demonstrated in Fig. 8(g). All ions, except for \(S O_{4}^{2-}\) at high concentrations, caused little change in the adsorption ability of the absorbent. The reason for this phenomenon might be that \(S O_{4}^{2-}\) could attract \(C\mathrm{d}^{2+}\) to affect the interfacial potential of the metal and subsequently limit its interaction with the adsorbent [182]. Nanoporous carbon materials from MOF are useful in the adsorption in \(C\mathrm{u}^{2+}\) due to large surface area and pore volume. MOF derived nanoporous carbon used to wipe off \(C\mathrm{u}^{2+}\) was investigated firstly in 2015. The result showed that the performance of commercial ACs for \(C\mathrm{u}^{2+}\) removal was not satisfactory. Nevertheless, MOF derived nanoporous carbon achieved better adsorption capacity due to large surface area and adequately graphitized. Its removal efficiency of \(C\mathrm{u}^{2+}\) was at least five times better than other commercial ACs (Fig. 8(h)). It exhibited the best capacity when \(\mathrm{pH}=5.0\) and temperature was \(60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). The excellent adsorption ability of MOF derived nanoporous carbon can be attributed to the existence of adequate functional groups on the surface [183]. Besides, the trace amount of \(\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{VI})\) can cause carcinogenic and mutation to humans and animals. UiO-66 can be utilized with polyaniline (PANI) to prepare N-doped carbon nanoparticles with large surface area \((1,609.1\;\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})\). The peak adsorption efficiency was \(198.04\;\mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\). The removal mechanism of \(\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{VI})\) was shown in Fig. 8(d). First, \(\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{VI})\) rapidly diffused to the surface of
PANI@NC-600 and was adsorbed by protonated \(-\mathrm{CH}_{2}\). Second, \(\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{VI})\) reacted with PANI and simultaneous transformed into less toxic \(\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{III})\) by the reduction of amine. Third, \(\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{III})\) can be further chelated with imino groups. To further evaluate the practical applicability, the capacity of PANI@NC-600 for \(\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{VI})\) removal in real water samples was studied. The removal efficiency did not show any obvious change in Dushu lake and Jinji lake water samples. Hence, PANI@NC-600 can be regarded as the excellent promising adsorbent for \(\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{VI})\) removal from waste water [184]. ZIF-67@nanoscale zero-valent iron (NZVI) derived NZVI@carbon showed good removal capacity for \(\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{VI})\). Because its large specific surface area and pore size were helpful when diffusion and removal of \(\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{VI})\). The peak adsorption efficiency of NZVI@carbon for \(\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{VI})\) was unexpectedly up to \(226.5~\mathrm{mg\cdot g^{-1}}\). In this process, the mesoporous carbon shell adsorbs \(\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{VI})\) through electrostatic attraction and further diffusions into the internal, where it could contact the NZVI core and caused the redox reaction (Fig. 8(b)) [185]. MOF derived \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.72}^{0}\mathrm{Fe}_{2.28}^{0}\mathrm{C}\) was also utilized to eliminate \(\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{VI})\), showing ideal removal capability with a peak efficiency up to \(354.6~\mathrm{mg\cdot g^{-1}}\). The formation of Fe with low valence including \(\mathrm{Fe}^{0}\) and \(\mathrm{Fe}^{(0)}\) was proved to be favorable for better efficiencies [186]. A \(\mathrm{Cu}/\) carbon composite was conveniently prepared by simple pyrolysis of a \(\mathrm{Cu}\) -based MOF, HKUST-1. The mechanism for reduction of \(\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{VI})\) was vividly shown in Fig. 8(c). With the assistance of HCOOH and \(\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}\), the composite showed good performance in reduction reaction, accomplishing it in a few minutes through catalytic reduction [187].
Apart from toxic heavy metals, carbon materials are effective in radionuclide removal in aqueous solution [188–190]. For example, a neotype MOFs derived PC showed enhanced capacity for the treatment of U(VI). They observed the removal ability of U(VI) to hit the peak when \(\mathrm{pH}\) was in the range of 3.0–3.8. During which, the electrostatic interaction between U(VI) and the negative charge on the surface of \(\mathrm{MgO}/\) carbon strengthened. Besides, it was found that the U(VI) removal efficiency of the carbon composite weakened by degrees with increased concentration of \(\mathrm{CO}_{3}{}^{2-}\). The increase of \(\mathrm{CO}_{3}{}^{2-}\) will raise the \(\mathrm{pH}\) and subsequently majority U(VI) present in the form of \((\mathrm{UO}_{2})_{2}\mathrm{CO}_{3}(\mathrm{OH})_{3}{}^{-}\) and \(\mathrm{UO}_{2}(\mathrm{CO}_{3})_{3}{}^{4-}\). These ions possessed large ionic diameters, high solubility, and strong electrostatic repulsion as well as fine mobility in solution, thus can signally weaken the removal capacity. According to the above explanation, the possible mechanisms for removing U(VI) were summed as surface coordination and electrostatic interaction (Fig. 8(a)) [191].
In short, it is believed that MOF derived carbon materials can provide many opportunities in a wide range of applications as adsorbents for metal ions.
## 3.2 Absorption of organic compounds
MOF derived carbon exhibits a remarkable treatment efficiency in organic compounds [11, 192–196]. For example, MOF derived carbon was obtained after \(24~\mathrm{h}\) with improved the adsorption efficiency of naphthalene around 17, which was 2.5 times higher than those of original MAF-6 and AC, respectively [193]. ZIF-8 was converted into PC after thermal decomposition at different temperatures (800, 1,000, and \(1,200\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\)). These PCs derived from MOF were recorded as PCDMs-800, PCDMs-1000, and PCDMs1200. Then the comparison was experimented on the adsorption capacity for ibuprofen and diclofenac sodium. They drew the conclusion that surface areas had effects on adsorptive performance because the \(q_{\mathrm{r}}\) -values were in good accordance with the sequence of surface area: PCDM-800: \(885~\mathrm{m^{2}{\cdot}g^{-1}}<\mathrm{PCDM-}\) 1200: \(1,590~\mathrm{\m^{2}{\cdot}g^{-1}}<\mathrm{PCDM-1000}\) : \(1,885~\mathrm{\m^{2}{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) (Figs. 9(b) and 9(c)). However, the non-linear tendency (Figs. 9(d) and 9(e)) of
the plots for ibuprofen and diclofenac sodium revealed that some special mutual effects can contribute to the difference in adsorption. Therefore, further adsorption experiments were taken in the same order over the produced PCDM-1000, pristine MOF, and commercial AC [13]. Another research utilized BMDCs produced under various pyrolysis time. They discovered that the product obtained after \(12~\mathrm{h}\) calefaction (BMDC-12 h) was the optimized adsorbent with best efficient for bisphenol A (BPA) capturing, followed by BMDC-24 h and BMDC-6 h, which was consistent with the order of surface areas [11]. Liang's team noticed that the specific surface areas for the N-PC and N-doped hollow porous carbon (NHPC-1 and NHPC-2) were 1,132.39, 1,032.91, and \(768.93~\mathrm{~m^{2}{\cdot}g^{-1}}\). Inversely, the tetracycline (TC) adsorptioncapacities@N-PCNHPC-1andNHPC-2wered80.2284.9and \(518.1~\mathrm{{\mg{\cdot}g^{-1}}}\), respectively, following the order of \(\mathrm{NHPC-2}~>\) \(\mathrm{NHPC-1}>\mathrm{N-PC}\). This result suggested that the TC adsorption capacity was also influenced by other factors like the rest major impetus for TC adsorption rather than simple contribution of the surface areas. First, lager pore size, more mesoporous, and fewer microporous structures lowered the TC steric hindrance, consequently making it easier to access to the pores of NHPC-2. Second, NHPC-2 contained abundant N, which led to stronger TC adsorption because it increased the surface N functional groups. Third, the functional groups in NHPC-2 including \(\pi{-}\pi\) interactions and the formation of H-bonding are in favor for TC adsorption (Fig. 9(a)) [31]. Other studies found that porous carbon framework obtained from ZIF-8 reached up to higher adsorption capacity due to the higher graphitic N content [29, 197].
Apart from N content, the amount of other heteroatoms can also attribute to the adsorption performance of the obtained carbon. For example, one research used ZIF-8 to obtain N-PC and N-doped carbon nanotubes, respectively. Interestingly, the N content of N-PC (15.20 at.%) was overwhelmingly higher than N-doped carbon nanotubes (2.87 at.%). However, the latter performed better in the experiment. One explanation was that the introduction of cobalt atoms in N-doped carbon nanotubes can boost the degree of graphitization of carbon networks and make it favorable for electron flowing. Furthermore, the close connections between Co and adjacent graphitic carbon layers can optimize the work efficiency of electronic and promote the catalytic performance [30].
In order to further explore the relationship between the organic micropollutants (OMPs) and the sorption behaviors of MOF-derived carbon, five OMPs including atrazine, carbamazepine (CBZ), bisphenol A, norfloxacin, and 4-nitrophenol were selected to test the adsorption performance of the MOF-derived magnetic porous carbon (MPCS). In the Figs. 9(g) and 9(h), the adsorption abilities of the five OMPs were positively correlated with the original concentration and the increase in adsorption rate flattened out by degrees and reached adsorption saturation eventually. We can clearly see that the performances of different OMPs kept to the sequence of atrazine \(<\mathrm{CBZ}<\) bisphenol A \(<\) norfloxacin \(<4\) -nitrophenol. Here are several reasons account for this sequence. First, \(\pi{-}\pi\) electron donor–acceptor interaction was enormously useful in the adsorption. At a neutral environment, the abundant \(-\mathrm{OH}\) groups made MPCs electron donors. CBZ was a weak \(\pi\) -donors due to the existence of \(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) group. Bisphenol A molecular was also a weak \(\pi\) -donor with two \(-\mathrm{OH}\) groups. On the contrary, norfloxacin molecular is active in accepting \(\pi\) electron because of the fluorine group's strong power to withdraw electron. 4-Nitrophenyl served as a stronger \(\pi\) - acceptor due to its lower electron density induced by electron-sucking group \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\). The theory had been proved that the interactions between a \(\pi\) -donor substance and a \(\pi\) -acceptor
substance were more intense. As a result, the efficiency of MPCSto absorb 4-nitrophenyl was markedly higher than that of other OMPs. Second, molecular electrostatic attraction was also a contributor to the adsorption order. In an almost neutral environment, 4- nitrophenol molecules were in dissociation and negatively charged while others were in their molecules forms. The 4-nitrophenol and MPCs had positively charged surface, so their attractions were much stronger, causing the peak adsorption of 4-nitrophenol. Third, hydrogen bond has also been utilized as a minor factor. Because of the existence of oxygen functional groups in MPCs, hydrogen bonds may present between MPCs and organic pollutants with some specific functional groups including OH, \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\), or NH. Different OMPs possessed this kind of functional groups in different amounts, thus making a great difference in their processing efficiency in the MPCs [86]. Later, several studies found that increasing N containing in the products can influence adsorption since porosity commonly decides the adsorption capacity. To better understand this theory, one research obtained PC with high N content from the pyrolysis of MOF by adding an ionic liquid (IL) \((\mathrm{IL}@\mathrm{MOF})\). Subsequently, the adsorption performances of MOF-derived carbon and IL@MOF-derived carbon were respectively measured to detect their applications in water purification. Figures 9(i) and 9(j) showed the adsorption curve of indole and quinolone over different adsorbents. Both two pictures demonstrated the same inclusion that IL@MOF-derived carbon-1000 (12%), with the maximum N content, exhibited excellent adsorption performance than any other carbon materials. The author explained that doped N appeared in the formation of N-6, N-5, and N-Q. Except N-Q had little effects on the
adsorption capacity, N-6 and N-5 sites were active and fully exhibited advantages in H-bonding formation [198].
In summary, MOF derived carbon is a potential adsorbent for the removal of various organic compounds owing to its high adsorption capacity, rapid capture, and facile recyclability.
## 3.3 Absorption of emerging pollution
As the population and urbanization generally pick up pace, some new pollutions are polluting water resources such as surface water and groundwater [199]. Medical drug residues and pharmaceutical or personal care products (PPCPs) are typical ones [200, 201]. These new emerging contaminants have drawn great attention because their endocrine, hormonal, and genetic may influence nature negatively [202]. As a result, in the face of generally serious new pollutants, purification of contaminated water has been carried out. Among all adsorption materials, MOF derived carbon is hot owing to its large and controllable porosity, well-defined pore structure, and decorated N (when MOF precursors contain N). NPC doped with Co particles (Co/NPC particles) was prepared through simple pyrolysis of ZIF-67 templates successfully. They discovered that magnetic Co nanoparticles were well embedded into ZIF-derived nanoporous carbon after the carbonization, showing good adsorption capacity for the rapid spread of methylene blue (MB). The products' adsorption capacities in different initial MB concentrations and the adsorption capacities of products obtained under different carbonization temperatures were studied. On the one hand, Co/NPC-800 exhibited a surprising adsorption performance of MB dye around \(500~\mathrm{mg\cdot g^{-1}}\), indicating its overwhelming
advantages over Co/NPC-600. On the other hand, Co/NPC-800 held outstanding adsorption capacities for a long time, showing its potential as an ideal material for purification of MB dye. To demonstrate the excellent performance of Co/NPC-800 more vividly, Fig. 10(a) recorded the color changes during the adsorption process. Once adding Co/NPC-800, the solution was almost transparent. Such high efficiency owed to the adulteration of Co particles. The MB molecules can get to the surface of Co particles and further insert into the Co/NPC product immediately, providing discontinuous active sites for MB adsorption [134].
Lin's team analyzed the impact external conditions on the adsorption capacity of Co/NPC. \(250\;\mathrm{mg\cdot L^{-1}}\) oxone was mixed with the solution and went through agitation at \(25~^{\circ}\!C\) for \(20\,\mathrm{\min}\) . Approximately \(12\%\) of oxone was used up to bleach rhodamine B (Rh.B) and the sample got mildly shallow. After this operation, the residual of Rh.B was measured around \(30~\mathrm{mg\cdot L^{-1}}\) . Subsequently, \(50~\mathrm{\mg\cdotL^{-1}}\) magnetic carbonaceous nanocomposite (MCN) was added and agitated for \(1\,\mathrm{{h}}\) at \(25~^{\circ}\!C,\) then the shiny red liquid was faded to colorless. This experiment indicted that oxone could barely bleach Rh.B, but the removal efficiency was fully improved with the addition of MCN (Fig. 10(c)). Second, the influence of temperature was studied, as exhibited in Fig. 10(d). The decolorization kinetics was remarkably enhanced along with increasing temperature. When at \(25~^{\circ}\!C,\) the time spent in total decolorization was about \(90\;\mathrm{min}\) . Once at \(50\;^{\circ}\!C,\) it was greatly cut down to \(5~\mathrm{min}\) and at \(80~^{\circ}\!C\) was only \(1\ \mathrm{min}\) . It was found that, after further study, the \(k\) value significantly raised from 0.054 to \(0.573\,\mathrm{\min^{-1}}\) when the temperature was from 25 to \(50~^{\circ}\!C.\) At \(80\ ^{\circ}{\cal C},\) the \(k\) value was markedly increased to \(4.4\,\mathrm{\min^{-1}}\) . In conclusion, higher temperature greatly promotes the occurrence
of MCN-activated oxone reaction. Third, \(\mathrm{pH}\) of solution was also analyzed as vitally influencing the oxone activation. It was clear that the efficiency was almost unchanged and remained high in the range of \(\mathrm{pH}\,=\,3{-}9\) . However, it was dramatically dropped when the initial \(\mathrm{pH}\) improved to 10 and 11 (Fig. 10(e)). This chart revealed that the decolorization process was quite stable and effective in acidic or neutral environment. The following decrease can be attributed to the self-decomposition of oxone in the alkaline condition. Last but not the least, the durability of MCN for the albibaction of Rh.B was also further studied. After 3 times of decoloration, the adsorption removal of MCN was still above \(95\%\) , revealing that MCN was a serviceable and high-efficiency material in decolorization treatment (Fig. 10(f) [203].
Apart from dyes, MOF derived carbon has also been proved as an ideal candidate for removing a series of medical drug residues out of water due to the excellent adsorption efficiency, short adsorption time, and fine stability. For instance, NPCs from a simple carbonization of ZIF-8 exhibited an optimal performance for ciprofloxacin removal from water. In order to determine the best ciprofloxacin adsorption efficiency, they investigated adsorbent dose, solution \(\mathrm{pH},\) time of contact, and original ciprofloxacin concentration, respectively. First, different amounts of the obtained NPC adsorbent were added to \(50\;\mathrm{mL}\) of \(10\;\mathrm{mg\cdotL^{-1}}\) ciprofloxacin to explore the effect of adsorbent amount. Figure \(10(\mathrm{g})\) showed the above two were negatively correlated because adsorption ability descended from 160.01 to \(87.46~\mathrm{mg\cdotg^{-1}}\) with the amount of NPC changed from 0.02 to \(0.10\;\mathrm{g\cdotL^{-1}}\) . In terms of \(\mathrm{pH}\) and contact time, the adsorption capacity reached the top at \(\mathrm{pH}\,=\,6\) and contact time \(=~21\) h. Moreover, the adsorption capacity of ciprofloxacin was positively related with ciprofloxacin
concentration (Fig. 10(h)). The reason for this phenomenon was probably that the higher initial ciprofloxacin concentration led to higher concentration gradient and higher driving force. In conclusion, these results demonstrated the essential application of the NPCs for environmental pollution cleanup [80]. Such high efficiency was also reflected in the adsorption of CBZ due to the presence of NaCl during the calcination of MOFs. The team fabricated hierarchically structured carbon composites (HCCs) with ZIF-8 as a sacrifice template appended with molten NaCl. To check the practical application of HCCs, the team utilized tap water containing \(10~\mathrm{mg\cdot L^{-1}}\) CBZ and controlled the flow velocity at \(3~\mathrm{mL\cdot min^{-1}}\) to conduct fixed-bed column tests. First and foremost, the adsorption ability of CBZ on HCCs in deionized water was a little higher than that in the tap water due to the influence of the ionic strength in the tap water. In the next place, Fig. 10(i) exhibited the CBZ breakthrough curve through the columns with HCCs, CPs, and AC. In the beginning of the curve, the residual CBZ increased slowly while the adsorption rate was quite fast due to the abundant active sites on the sorbents. The remanent CBZ then increased linearly and finally reached the saturation point as the active sites were occupied by degrees. No matter \(10\%\) or \(50\%\) concentration, HCCs showed the largest breakthrough volumes \((C_{0}/C_{t}=0.1)\) about 2,958 and 5,527 BV, respectively. This demonstrated that HCCs were promising adsorbents for practical removal of CBZ. Furthermore, they used solvent elution and heat-treatment to further evaluate the cyclicity. Figure 10(j) was the recovery about CBZ breakthrough curve via the fixed-bed column. Through the approach of methanol elution or pyrolysis, the \(10\%\) breakthrough volumes of the reused HCCs shrank to 469 and 765 BV, respectively. This implied the intense adsorption between CBZ and HCCs. So it was hard to separate them totally [80]. Pyrolysis can carbonize CBZ to release more sites but the obtained carbon blocked off the pores, lowering the circulation ability. In the end, the possible adsorption mechanisms were analyzed. For one thing, functional groups including –COOH in HCCs and \(\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\) in CBZ can fabricate hydrogen
bonds, leading to the enhanced adsorption. On the other hand, the \(\pi{-}\pi\) electron donor–acceptor interaction may be the principle reason for its great efficiency. \(\pi{-}\pi\) electron donor–acceptor interaction minified the electron density of HCCs, causing the stronger binding energy between carbon atoms and oxygen atoms (Fig. 10(b)). In summary, the impressive adsorption performance of HCCs confirmed that introducing chemical guests during the pyrolysis of MOFs was an viable way to optimize various features of MOF-derived PC, subsequently improving their adsorption capacity in adsorption treatment of pollutants from water [204].
In general, carbon materials from carbonization of MOF possess optimized morphology, microstructures, and other advantages, which make them be attractive adsorbents for the adsorptive removal of organics from water.
## 3.4 Capacitive deionization
CDI is a desalination technology with great promise for brackish water with low to medium salinity [205]. Carbon-based materials, including ACs, carbon nanocages, carbon nanorods, and carbon fibers, have been widely utilized in CDI process for their respective characteristics like large surface area, biologically inert, and good electrochemical stability. Table 2 lists some typical cases of carbon materials derived from MOFs applied in CDI. However, typical CDI processes using these electrodes show limited salt removal efficiency due to their poor electrical conductivity and ununiform pore shape and size [206]. Therefore, the exploration of novel carbon materials with better ability to capture saline ions is a breakthrough in capacitive deionization, laying the foundation for improving removal efficiency. So far, the desalination efficiency and circulation stability of MOF derivate carbon have been well optimized via several ways including morphology adjustment, heteroatom decorating, etc. [46]. In addition, these materials are not confronted with dilemmas such as expensive cost of manufacture and low operation efficiency in large-scale applications.
Chang's team reported a study about the performance in CDI
of different PCs prepared by direct carbonization of MOFs in different temperatures. In comparison with the commercially available AC, PCs originated from MOFs showed higher efficiency as follows. According to the data, the solution conductivity of PC and AC electrodes diminished dramatically in the start, then generally flattened out and achieved the state of balance after \(3\,\mathrm{h}\). The adsorption efficiency was 2.17, 4.05, 4.88, 9.39, and \(9.09\,\mathrm{mg\cdot g^{-1}}\) for PC-500, PC-700, PC-850, PC-900, and PC-1000 electrodes, respectively. As anticipated, PC-900 revealed the optimal absorption capacity, whose adsorption efficiency was better compared with AC electrode \((5.47\,\mathrm{mg\cdot g^{-1}})\). This study also threw light on the long cycling life of PCs. In the following investigation, PC-900 electrode was placed in \(500\,\mathrm{mg\cdot L^{-1}}\) NaCl with a sustaining potential of \(1.2\,\mathrm{V}\). The conductivity of liquid can fortify sharply after reversal of the cell potential and circuit opening. Moreover, the decrease in desalination efficiency did not show after 10 cycles, confirming their recycle stability of CDI process [82].
MOF derived carbon materials with characteristic structures show ascendant performance in CDI. For example, hollow structure provides abundant active sites and diminishs spread distance for better mass transport. Hollow ZIFs derived nanocarbon (HZCs) and solid ZIFs derived nanocarbon (SZCs) electrodes were put in NaCl solution with a commercial AC \((780.6\,\mathrm{cm^{2}\cdot g^{-1}})\) electrode as a contrast to compare their electrosorption performances (Fig. 11(b)). Obviously, the conductivity of HZCs and SZCs declined rapidly within \(10\,\mathrm{min}\) and subsequently smoothed to a steady plateau in the wake of the external voltage applied. As shown in Figs. 11(c) and 11(d), the data not only basically conformed to pseudo-first-order equation \((R_{2}>0.99)\), but also exhibited that HZCs possessed rapid ion
diffusion and highest electrosorption rate value. Figure 11(e) demonstrated the performance of gained electrodes at different initial concentrations in CDI. The absorption capacities of both electrodes increased with the augment of concentration. Meanwhile, HZCs electrodes performed better in all initial concentrations than SZCs ones due to their larger enclosed area. Subsequently, contact angle perfected wettability and conductivity to some extent. Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) results also demonstrated the predominant structure of HZCs, which was in favor of CDI efficiency. In conclusion, the abovementioned superior properties of HZCs in CDI mainly relied on the appropriate pore distribution and particular hollow structure [215].
Furthermore, heteroatoms can be equally distributed in MOF derived carbon, which are beneficial for improving the comprehensive performance of carbon materials such as activity, conductivity, hydrophilicity, and stability [218, 219]. For example, introducing heteroatoms (N, P, B, S, etc.) is deemed as another practical way to strengthen the chemical activity by modifying surface features and the band gap [105]. When it comes to N doped ones, their CDI performances are greatly improved attribute to moderate amount of N doping plus with a larger surface area and more proper pore size distribution. After successfully obtaining the N-PC from MOF with triethylenediamine as the N source, Zhang's team proved that this kind of material can be an ideal electrode candidate in CDI process. The experimental consequence showed that the removal ability can be up to \(24.17\,\mathrm{mg\cdot g^{-1}}\) in \(500\,\mathrm{mg\cdot L^{-1}}\) NaCl under \(1.2\,\mathrm{V}\) and was still in good conditions after more than 35 cycles [109]. Another article also demonstrated that the N-doped carbon tubes showed a peak salt removal efficiency about \(56.9\,\mathrm{mg\cdot g^{-1}}\) as well as
excellent cyclic durability. They found that these multiple advantages were the results of tubular architecture and plentiful N dopants. The former can diminish the diffusion distance between electrons and ions, while the latter can enhance the activity and conductivity of carbon matrix, due to both the unique electron transfer property of graphitic N dopants and the coordination effect of pyridinic N dopants [40]. Based on the synthesis of S doped PC described above, researchers further examined their CDI performances. From electrochemical test, S-doped mesoporous carbon held a steady durability of \(173.7\,\mathrm{\mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) under \(200\;\mathrm{mA{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) after 500 times and an expressive rate capability of 104.9 and \(90\;\mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) under 1.6 and \(3.2\;\mathrm{A{\cdot}g^{-1}}\), respectively. To explain the ameliorative properties, they analyzed the influence of the S atom on the material and summed up the following two points. First, because of the relatively bigger diameter of S atom, the distance between each interlayer was greatly increased to \(0.386\ \mathrm{nm}\) (Fig. 11(a)), setting the stage for accommodating more \(\mathrm{Na^{+}}\) to improve the ion storage. This also promoted the interposition and extraction by lowering the energy barrier when inserting \(\mathrm{Na^{+}}\). Second, S was an attractant for its plentiful active sites for storage [112].
Apart from adapting nonmetal heteroatoms as the dopants to enhance the adsorption performance of the MOF derivate carbon composites, metal heteroatoms are also good choices. The hybrid capacitive deionization (HCDI) was equipped with the \(\mathrm{Ag/ZCs}\) as anode electrode and HZCs as cathode electrode (Fig. 11(f)). According to Fig. 11(g), with the enhancement of applied voltage, the eventual balance conductivity of HCDI could diminish from 143.0 to \(122.4\;\;\upmu\mathrm{S}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\), which can demonstrate the gradually increasing deionization capacity in their opinion. And, higher applied voltage can lead to a thicker electric double layers (EDL) and more powerful electrostatic interactions, leading to the enhancement in capacitance characteristics. Moreover, Fig. 11(h) showed that \(\mathrm{Ag/ZCs}\) HCDI possessed the best performance, which may be attributed to that \(\mathrm{Ag^{+}}\) reduced from Ag nanoparticles under a higher applied voltage can attract more Cl- and subsequently optimize the battery performance in capacity in CDI process. In terms of the cycle efficiency (Figs. 11(i) and 11(j)), the \(\mathrm{Ag/ZCs}\) HCDI maintained fine working condition during the 20 electrosorption–desorption cycles with the highest deionization capacity up to \(18.74\;\mathrm{mg{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) and \(94.6\%\) of the original deionization efficiency [124].
In summary, MOF derived carbon is a potential electrode candidate for CDI. The larger exploring goal is to achieve faster treatment rate, better durability, better environmental friendliness, and so on.
## 3.5 Others
The good features originated from MOFs enable MOF derived carbon a promising future for the treatment of other water pollution. For instance, for their easy functionalization, adjustable pore size, high surface area, practical production, and so on, they were utilized for the adsorption of pesticides from aqueous [220]. PC derived by pyrolysis from ZIF-8 exhibited enhanced ability in effectively removing a pharmaceutical waste, much more effective than initial ZIF-8 [221]. Besides, MIL-101 derived carbon showed a maximum release of phosphate capacity of \(97.73\;\mathrm{mg{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) [222]. In terms of pesticide residues, liquid-incorporated ZIF-8 derived carbon exhibited maximal adsorption capacity of toxic herbicides diuron around \(284\;\mathrm{mg{\cdot}g^{-1}}\). This remarkable efficiency was probably because of its abundant number of active sites on the surface and relatively high porosity [223]. Moreover, ZIF-9 derived carbon composites obtained through heating at \(800\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) under \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) showed superior potential in CDI. The desalination efficiency reached \(55.4\;\mathrm{mg{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) under \(1.2\;\mathrm{V}\) when in \(584\;\mathrm{mg{\cdot}mL^{-1}}\) NaCl liquid. This provided a practical way for killing microorganisms. Through examination with colon bacillus and gram-positive rods, the amount of bacterial colonies was obviously decreased, showing the high inhibition ratio around \(95\%\) [224].
NaCl liquid. This provided a practical way for killing microorganisms. Through examination with colon bacillus and gram-positive rods, the amount of bacterial colonies was obviously decreased, showing the high inhibition ratio around \(95\%\) [224].
## 3.6 Summary
As technology develops, MOF derived carbon possessed novel morphology will be applied in practical use. Their respective characteristics determine different applications in various fields. By now, the development in the ingredient and morphology of MOF derived NPCs is utilizing zero-dimensional (0D) polyhedral to 1D rods [225, 226], 2D sheets [227–229], and 3D arrays [230, 231]. In terms of 0D, MOF derived carbon carbon nanodots exhibit promising advantages, such as excellent chemical stability, up-conversion emission, easy functionalization, pH-sensitivity, and resistance to photobleaching [232]. In comparison, 1D nanostructures, particularly those having porous and hollow structures, exhibit many merits such as high conductivity and stable structure [233, 234]. For example, carbon nanofibers and nanotubes offer good flexibility, large surface area, high porosity, and high conductivity. In addition, the homogeneous incorporation of heteroatoms and metallic constituents contribute to an abundant exposure of active nanocatalytic sites [235]. Besides, nanosheet shows great potential in water treatment since its bandgap characteristic can simultaneously accelerate the surface water oxidation kinetics [236]. 3D structure such as carbon nanocage provides large void space, further buffering the volume change and stabilizing the structure during charge–discharge [237].
## 4 Conclusion and outlooks
In summary, this review primarily introduces that MOFs are proved as excellent precursors to fabricate different MOF derived carbon, including porous carbon, nanocarbon, graphite, and their composites doped with no-metallic elements and metal elements. They inherit the advantages of their precursor MOFs including fine porosity, large surface area, plenty active sites, and adjustable structure/composition. The high performances associated with these carbon materials from MOFs are also intensively introduced. As highlighted in this review, heteroatom doping such as N, Co, and Fe has been proved to be feasible approaches for strengthening the electronic features of MOF derived carbon materials like speeding up charge transfer rate. These possibilities offer chances to develop MOF derived carbon materials with desired adsorption capacity and further enhance water purification efficiency. However, there are still many obstacles towards the widespread utilizations of these materials in various fields of water treatment. For example, it is difficult to effectively control the ratio of heteroatoms when introduced into MOFs precursor. Besides, more effort can be devoted for the exploration of other MOF precursor such as MIL. Moreover, the large-scale manufacture of MOF derived carbon faces adverse condition due to their low productivity (usually in the range of \(13\%{-}21\%\)) depending on the thermalization environment. Furthermore, the limitations in specific surface area and no hierarchically porosity of MOF derived carbon may exert negative effect on their maximum performances. Thereby, the following prospects are proposed:
(1) By introducing different heteroatom or functional groups or further modifying the crystalline structures, MOF derived carbon can provide more ultrahigh porosity and enriched active sites. So it enjoys greater potential in water treatment efficiency such as increasing charge transfer rate, shorter adsorption time, and improved adsorption capacity per unit volume.
(2) MOFs derived carbon contains untreated impurities, which may bring secondary pollution when utilized in water treatment. As a result, it is necessary to explore more efficiency methods for removing impurities after carbonization.
(3) Although most MOF precursors can be prepared under mild conditions, the practical applications of MOFs are limited due to commercial availability and high costs compared to other carbonaceous materials. As a result, more effort should be devoted to make MOF-derived carbon an economical option for practice application.
(4) More green approaches to synthesizing MOF-derived carbon should be developed, such as microwave, laser radiation, or other electrochemical and mechanical methods. Besides, combining MOF derived carbon with other basic materials (graphite oxide, metal oxide, etc.) to form hybrids can enhance their recyclability and reusability.
(5) By controlling the carbonization conditions such as temperature, duration time, gas atmosphere, and heating rate, it is possible to make the product better retain the order characteristics of the precursor, further optimizing its practical performance.
